<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=barbed-wire">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbed wire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as barb wire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barbed wire is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> fencing wire with sharp points set at intervals along its strands, used for cheap fences and, as a wire obstacle, in trench warfare. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Leonce Eugene Grassin-Baledans proposed a fence of thin wire with bristling points in 1860, and Louis François Janin patented a double wire with diamond-shaped barbs in 1865. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Lucien B. Smith of Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, received the first patent in 1867, and Joseph Glidden of DeKalb, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, patented the modern form in 1874. It made fencing the western plains cheap, and the open range was closed amid the Fence Cutting Wars of the 1880s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barbed-wire.jpg" alt="Barbed wire" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prosthetic Head · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rusted_barbed_wire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1865</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Louis François Janin · Lucien B. Smith · Joseph Glidden</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Lucien B. Smith, who received the first United States patent in 1867, is regarded as the inventor; Leonce Eugene Grassin-Baledans had proposed a pointed wire fence in France in 1860.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=barbed-wire">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
